NAME
    cp - copy a regular file

SYNOPSIS
    cp SRC DST

DESCRIPTION
    Copies SRC's contents to DST. DST is created (or truncated if it
    already exists). Two-argument form only — there is no
    `cp SRC... DSTDIR` multi-source mode and no -r recursive flag yet.

    The copy goes via a single contiguous buffer in the calling task's
    heap (sized via sys_brk), then SYS_FS_WRITE persists the buffer to
    DST in one atomic sector-run swap. An interrupted cp leaves DST
    either unchanged or fully replaced — never half-overwritten.

EXAMPLES
    cp hello.txt copy.txt
    cp /etc/passwd /tmp/passwd.bak

EXIT STATUS
    0   success
    1   any failure (SRC missing, DST permission denied, out of memory,
        short read on SRC, etc.)

SEE ALSO
    mv(1), rm(1), find(1)

NOTES
    No -p (preserve mode), -i (interactive), or -r (recursive). Added
    in session 83. See docs/70-usable-unix-coreutils.md for the
    coreutils gap-fill design.
